    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Fossils are strictly found in Sedimentary rocks; Igneous and Metamorphic rocks do not preserve organic remains.",
        "Remember parent-to-metamorphic pairs: Limestone becomes Marble, Sandstone becomes Quartzite, Shale becomes Slate, and Granite becomes Gneiss.",
        "The Deccan Traps of India are composed of Basalt, an extrusive igneous rock formed by fissure lava eruptions."
      ],
      "id": "3rrnkqx"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Igneous rocks are formed by the cooling and solidification of molten magma or lava and lack organic fossils.",
        "Sedimentary rocks are formed through the lithification of accumulated sediments and frequently contain fossils within strata.",
        "Metamorphic rocks develop from pre-existing rocks undergoing structural and mineralogical changes due to severe heat and dynamic pressure.",
        "Extrusive igneous rocks cool rapidly on the Earth's surface creating fine-grained textures, whereas intrusive igneous rocks cool slowly underground forming coarse grains.",
        "Metamorphism alters rock texture and composition, producing foliated structures like gneiss or non-foliated structures like quartzite."
      ],
      "id": "cmscci8"
    }
